 118: def main(grid: np.ndarray) -> np.ndarray:
 119:     """Drop paired colored markers inside a grey lattice, then color-code the post-gravity alignments with connector trails.
 120: 
 121:     Input structure:
 122:     - The grid is a regular grey lattice whose full grey rows and columns divide the canvas into black channel cells.
 123:     - Inside the channel cells are isolated single-pixel markers; each marker color appears exactly twice.
 124:     - Grey lattice pixels are fixed obstacles and all remaining non-lattice pixels are background channels.
 125: 
 126:     Rule:
 127:     - Move every marker straight downward through black channel pixels, processing lower markers first and stopping above the first lattice pixel, the bottom edge, or an already settled marker.
 128:     - After gravity, inspect each same-colored pair: if the two landed on the same row, paint yellow through the background pixels strictly between them; if they landed on the same column, paint magenta through the background pixels strictly between them.
 129:     - Keep grey lattice pixels, marker pixels, and any off-path background pixels unchanged.
 130: 
 131:     Key insight:
 132:     - The connector color is not determined from the input endpoints directly; gravity inside the lattice changes where the endpoints land, and only those landed positions decide whether the pair is row-aligned, column-aligned, or unconnected.
 133:     """
 134:     return _transform(grid)
