 148: def main(grid: np.ndarray) -> np.ndarray:
 149:     """Split separator-crossing color objects into band fragments, drop each band, and keep only the densest band filled.
 150: 
 151:     Input structure:
 152:     - The grid has black background and exactly two full-width red separator rows that partition it into three horizontal bands.
 153:     - Foreground objects use distinct colors from {blue, green, orange, teal}; some colors appear in more than one band because a separator visually slices that object.
 154:     - Separator rows contain no object pixels and remain fixed barriers.
 155: 
 156:     Rule:
 157:     - Treat each band independently: every non-black, non-red pixel falls straight downward within its own column until it reaches the band floor or another settled pixel.
 158:     - Count the total foreground pixels in each band after this band-wise gravity.
 159:     - Keep the unique densest band, recolor every remaining black cell in that band to grey, and clear the foreground pixels from the other two bands to black.
 160:     - Leave both red separator rows unchanged.
 161: 
 162:     Key insight:
 163:     - The red lines do not move; they split any multi-band color object into separate band fragments, so the winner is determined only by the post-split per-band pixel counts.
 164:     """
 165:     return _solve(np.asarray(grid, dtype=int))
